    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faults and related damage zones influence the movement of fluids in the subsurface. A detailed knowledge of the distribution of these structures is there- fore required for an accurate assessment of their influence. Previous studies have attempted to characterize fault property distributions (e.g., fault rock thickness, damage zone width) using data combined from faults formed by different faulting mechanisms. An earlier study at Stanford (Myers, 1999) utilized geometric parameters obtained from either single points or selected scanlines across faults formed by a particular mechanism. This study builds upon the previous study with the examination of a single large fault along its entire exposed length (2 km). The data gathered reflects the high degree of variability present along a single structure. Plots of fault rock thickness versus fault slip show no clear trend. This new data raises questions for the applicability of a universal fault scaling law and necessitates a new approach utilizing empirical, geomechanical, and geostatistical methods to characterizing fault property distributions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
